//! check: run(exit=trap(assert))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.random.fill, os.random.trap, conf.trap.map
//!
//! The OS random source (s118, wolf-lang#143) — the trap witness.
//! `os_random(-1)` is a caller contract violation: the deterministic
//! trap `assert` on every lane ([os.random.fill], the [mem.str.repeat]
//! posture) — never an empty list, never a row. This file also
//! witnesses the trap PLUMBING of [os.random.trap]: the surface has no
//! error row at all, so the runtime's nonzero code has exactly one
//! spelling — trap(assert) — and the OS-failure path (an entropy
//! source the platform cannot provide) rides the SAME nonzero-rc
//! branch witnessed here. That path itself cannot be forced
//! deterministically from a corpus program (a host whose CSPRNG fails
//! is not a thing a test can arrange), which is why the caller-contract
//! trap is the honest executable witness of the shared mechanism.

fn main() -> !int {
    let b = os_random(-1)
    b.len
}
